00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.760
Hi, I'm Majim Rahman, a graduate

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.680
researcher from the department of

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.000
industrial engineering at the University

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.320
of Pittsburgh where I worked with Dr.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.080
Lou and Dr. Rahimeian to recover solar

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.679
cells and bicial panels.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.680
Solar energy is one of the fastest

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:19.439
growing renewable energy technologies.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.439
However, solar panels do not last

00:00:19.439 --> 00:00:22.439
forever.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.599
Most modules have a lifetime of about 25

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:29.279
to 30 years. Then as solar deployment

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:33.720
continues to increase, the amount of end

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:33.720
of life PV waste is growing rapidly.

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:38.000
Many end of life panels are landfilled

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.160
and current recycling often recovers

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.480
only raw materials while destroying high

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.480
value components.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.440
So this leads to our key research

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.440
question. Can intex silicon wafers be

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.440
recovered?

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.399
To answer this question, this work

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.480
focuses on end of life bicial glass

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.760
solar panels. These modules are

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.760
important because they feature high

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.719
efficiency cells with glass layers on

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.719
the front and the back.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.000
The main goal is to separate the panel

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.880
layers and recover valuable materials,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.640
especially intact silicon wafers because

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:17.320
they have higher use potential than

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.320
broken or fully processed silicon waste.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.600
The key step is thermal elimination. And

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.159
during wafer recovery, samples are

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.000
heated to 500° C to remove the EVA

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:28.400
encapsulant and separate the panel

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:32.840
layers. The removal of clean EVA is

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.840
essential for the recovery of induct.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.280
After the encapsulant is removed, it

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.799
becomes easier to separate the different

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.880
layers by allowing the recovery of

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:44.079
materials including glass, aluminum,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.840
copper, and notably silicon wafers. The

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:48.240
experimental results revealed that

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.079
recovering intact wafers is feasible

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:54.520
particularly when the samples have

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:54.520
little or no pomech mechanical damage.

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:59.040
Next, we evaluated the environmental

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.119
impact using life cycle assessment. The

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.960
results indicate a significant reduction

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:04.240
in the cumulative energy demand when

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.880
transitioning from laboratory to

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.319
industrial scale. However, thermal

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.319
treatment remains the dominant energy

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:11.319
contributor.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.720
Finally, we evaluated the physibility of

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.120
the strategy from an economic

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.720
standpoint. The analysis shows that

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.200
recovered wafers can reduce model

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.480
manufacturing cost from 10.5 cents per

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:28.480
watt peak to 9.2 cents per watt peak.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.920
And when more than half of the wafers

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.800
are fully recovered, the technique

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.800
becomes commercially viable.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.280
So our study shows that end ofly bfacial

00:02:37.519 --> 00:02:41.200
solar panels can be transformed into

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.920
valuable sources of recoverable glass

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.239
and metals as well as reusable silicon

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.879
wafers supporting circular economic

00:02:46.239 --> 00:02:50.560
principles. So in summary, end of life

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:52.879
solar panels do not have to become the

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.800
waste. They have the potential to serve

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:58.120
as valuable assets for future solar

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.120
module development.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.959
Thank


